
# function with-pipefail: run a command and return its exit status,
# but set -o pipefail first (and restore the original value of
# pipefail afterwards).
#
# Explanation for those who haven't had the displeasure of dealing
# with pipefail:
#
# In Unix, if a command's exit status is 0, this indicates success,
# and is true in a Boolean context. Any value other than 0 indicates
# an error, and is false in a Boolean context.
# 
# Bash works the same way. After you call a function, its exit status
# will be in the special variable $?, like this:
#
# user@localhost:~$ /usr/bin/true
# user@localhost:~$ echo $?
# 0
#
# user@localhost:~$ /usr/bin/false
# user@localhost:~$ echo $?
# 1
#
# user@localhost:~$ if /usr/bin/true || /usr/bin/false; then echo "True"; else echo "False"; fi
# True
#
# user@localhost:~$ if /usr/bin/true && /usr/bin/false; then echo "True"; else echo "False"; fi
# $ if /usr/bin/true && /usr/bin/false; then echo "True"; else echo "False"; fi
# False
#
# user@localhost:~$ echo "splendid" > ~/tmp/text-file
# user@localhost:~$ grep "nonexistent string" ~/tmp/text-file
# user@localhost:~$ echo $?
# 1
#
# So far, so good. However - and this is the important part, and the motivation
# for writing the function 'with-pipefail in the first place - Bash behaves
# in the following counterintuitive way:
# 
# user@localhost:~$ grep "nonexistent string" ~/tmp/text-file | less
# user@localhost:~$ echo $?
# 0
#
# Wait, what? Why does this command give exit status 0 (true)
# when we know that grep returned 1 (false)?
# 
# The answer is that, counterintuitively, a bash command with a pipe ('|')
# will poppulate $? with the exit status of the *last command in the pipeline*,
# which in this case is 'less', which succeeds at its own job (paginating
# the output grep), and gives exit status 1.
#
# This is probably not the behavior that you expect or want.
# Probably you want the whole pipeline to indicate failure,
# with the exit status of the pipeline being nonzero.
# The way to achieve this is to run this command:
#
# set -o pipefail
#
# and *then* run this: grep "nonexistent string" ~/tmp/text-file | less
# which will return 1, the exit status of grep.
#
# More generally, if you have a pipeline of many commands, 'set -o pipefail'
# ensures that the exit status will be that of the leftmost command
# that fails.
#
# Great! We have a fix for Bash's weird default semantics!
#
# However, if we 'set -o pipefail' inside a function, that will
# set it globally for the duration of the Bash process, which
# may mess up the caller of our function (if they wrote their own
# code to assume the default behavior... which is obviously
# a reasonable thing to do).
# 
# Therefore, if you want to 'set -o pipefail' inside a function,
# you must first record the original value of pipefail and then
# reset it at the end (unless pipefail was already on, in which
# case you don't need to do anything).
